<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Robert Bosch</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1861">1861</a>–<a href="/years/1942">1942</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Robert Bosch was a German engineer and industrialist from Württemberg. He opened a workshop for precision mechanics and electrical engineering in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stuttgart">Stuttgart</a> in 1886 and made his fortune with magneto ignition systems for motor engines. His firm grew into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-bosch-gmbh">Robert Bosch GmbH</a>, one of the world's largest suppliers of automotive parts. He was known for progressive treatment of workers and for charitable foundations that now own most of the company.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/robert-bosch.jpg" alt="Robert Bosch" width="320" height="441" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Robert_Bosch,_1925.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Robert Bosch was born on 23 September 1861 at Albeck in the Swabian Highlands near Ulm, one of twelve children of Servatius Bosch, who ran a large progressive farm with a brewery. He attended the Realanstalt in Ulm until 1879, with an apprenticeship as a precision-instrument maker. In 1883 and 1884 he studied at the Stuttgart Technical University. On 24 May 1884 he sailed for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and worked as an engineer under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-edison">Thomas Edison</a> and Sigmund Bergmann in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>; in 1885 he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, where he found employment with Siemens Brothers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>On 15 November 1886 Bosch opened his own Workshop for Precision Mechanics and Electrical Engineering in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stuttgart">Stuttgart</a>. In 1887 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gottlieb-daimler">Gottlieb Daimler</a> asked whether he could build a device like the low-voltage magneto with firing points used by the Gasoline Engine Factory Deutz, to produce the spark that ignites the air-fuel mixture at maximum compression, and Bosch made four. In 1893 Frederick Richard Simms proposed adapting the stationary-engine magneto for motor vehicles. Arnold <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-zahringen">Zähringer</a> and Gottlob Honold modified it to raise the speed of sparks from 250 to 1800 rpm, and orders from Daimler followed after a demonstration in January 1898.</p><p>By 1913 the company had branches in America, Asia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> and made 88% of its sales outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. After the First World War Bosch introduced diesel fuel injection in 1927, and in the 1920s he modernised and diversified, turning a small automotive supplier into a multinational electronics group.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Bosch was one of the first German industrialists to introduce the eight-hour day, and he promoted occupational training. He did not wish to profit from armaments contracts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> and gave several million marks to charity, including the Robert Bosch Hospital in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stuttgart">Stuttgart</a>. As a liberal he devoted energy and money to reconciliation between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. His relationship with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazism">Nazism</a> is described as complex: as much as 60% of the firm's revenue came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wehrmacht">Wehrmacht</a> contracts in the 1940s, yet he worked with Carl Friedrich Goerdeler, central to the plot of 20 July, and his managers joined the Nazi party to keep committed Nazis out of the company.</p><p>In 1937 he made the company a private limited company, and his will required that its earnings go to charitable causes. He died on 12 March 1942, survived by four children from two marriages, and was inducted into the Automotive Hall of Fame in 1984.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Robert Bosch” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By the end of the century Stuttgart had become a centre of engineering: Gottlieb Daimler and Wilhelm Maybach built petrol engines in Cannstatt, <b>Robert Bosch</b> opened his workshop in Stuttgart in 1886, and Count Ferdinand von Zeppelin flew his first rigid airship over Lake Constance in 1900.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-wurttemberg">Kingdom of Württemberg · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Albeck, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stuttgart">Stuttgart</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.48,10.06&amp;year=1913" aria-label="Albeck on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4783 469 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Robert Bosch"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5233 739h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5233 739h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.48,10.06&amp;year=1913">What was here in 1913? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
